\section*{ABSTRACT}

\title{
Sociodemographic Predictors of Patient Adherence to Timely Follow-up Colposcopy/LEEP After Abnormal PAP Screening
}

\author{
GIGI BAER \\ McGovern Medical School at UTHealth \\ Class of 2028 \\ Sponsored by: \\ Abigail S. Zamorano, MD, MPHS, Department of Gynecologic Oncology at MD Anderson \\ Supported by: \\ Dean's Contribution Award \\ Key Words: \\ Pap testing, social determinants of health
}

Background: While cervical cancer incidence has declined with screening, its benefit requires timely follow-up (FU) of abnormal results. Limited data exists on how social determinants of health (SDOH) impact FU. The Houston PAP Project (HPAP) provides free cervical cancer screening (CCS) and dysplasia diagnosis/management. We sought to examine SDOH association with timely care.
Methods: In this retrospective cohort analysis, patients completed SDOH surveys at the time of CCS. Time from abnormal CCS to colposcopy (diagnosis) and LEEP (management) were calculated, where timely was defined as the first-third quartiles and delayed as the fourth. High risk (HR) patients had CCS results of ASC-H, HSIL, AGC, or HPV 16/18; low risk (LR) patients had ASCUS or LSIL. Chi-square and t-tests assessed associations between SDOH and FU time.
Results: Between 1/2022-5/2025, 77 patients were recommended colposcopy and 22 LEEP. Mean age was 42.0y; most were Hispanic (90.0\%), Spanish-speaking (95.0\%), and uninsured (91.3\%). Most (73.8\%) had at least one SDOH need: literacy (23.9\%), transportation (30.0\%), housing (35.0\%), food (50.0\%), finance (22.5\%), or stress (42.5\%). FU rates were high: 89.6\% colposcopy; 81.0\% LEEP. Of the rest, one was unreachable, and others sought care elsewhere. Median FU was 49d (SD 75.3) for colposcopy and 56d (SD 34.2) for LEEP. Among LR \((\mathrm{n}=45)\), financial SDOH was associated with longer FU (146.2 vs 48.5d, \(\mathrm{p}=0.02\) ). LEEP at a different HPAP location was associated with sooner FU (42.7 vs 86.3d, \(\mathrm{p}=0.01\) ).
Conclusion: Financial SDOH was associated with delayed FU among LR, but not HR patients, and LEEP at a different location had sooner FU than at the same location. As LEEP is recommended for high-grade dysplasia, these findings suggest some may choose to FU more quickly after education on the severity of the condition. This highlights the importance of education and the need for improved accessibility.

